Fold the schedule, and letter the design under the reader

The schedule of spot heights took a lot of the sheet: 144 rows on a fine
ground, and because `.schedule` folds each row into a block at 620px, some
five hundred lines of table on a phone under two drawings. It is shut by
default now — 17px of summary against the 3,875px it stands at open — and it
keeps every row for the reader who wants to compare them.

What makes the fold legitimate is the line that now stands in view. The
schedule's three jobs are all questions about one design at a time, which is
how a survey is read; the table answered them by printing all 144 answers at
once. `renderSpotReadout` answers them where they are asked, lettered by
pointing at a tick or by walking the ground with the arrow keys.

  - The plan letters only every second position on each axis once a ground
    passes seven, so 108 of a fine ground's 144 figures are lettered nowhere
    on it. Measured on a 12 x 12 ground with 49 positions landed: 49 ticks
    drawn, 4 figures on the plan.
  - Both drawings are role="img", which makes the whole subtree
    presentational, so a shut table is no route to a figure for assistive
    technology and neither was an open one — surveyAriaLabel carries no
    reading. The readout is role="status", the live region the cursor's
    sentence never had: surveySay writes into the refusal paragraph and a
    refusal is not announced. The refusal paragraph now carries only refusals.
  - The relief can refuse to draw (FR-024), and the plan plus this line are
    then the survey without anything needing to be opened.

"Readings never go in a fold" is not broken by this and the distinction is
worth keeping: that rule is about the reading the page is for, which stays in
view — the readout, the coverage line, the plan's own figures. The fold holds
the record, which is a different thing, and its summary carries the count so a
reader who never opens it still knows what it holds. Same shape as the TM59
qualifications block.

A gap's reason is lettered now too, rather than living only in a <title>,
which pointer: coarse never shows.

Three things that needed getting right. The <details> is static markup and
only its table is rebuilt, because renderSurvey runs on every landed sample
and a rebuilt fold would slam shut under a reader who had opened it — the
hazard that made renderSurveyChoose stop rebuilding the extent fields;
verified the node survives six redraws while the table refilled 49 to 144
rows. A hover does not move the keyboard cursor and re-renders nothing. And
pointerleave is honoured only for a mouse: a touch pointer is destroyed on
release, so a symmetrical handler lettered a tapped design and took it away
again in the same gesture.

Costs 1,420 bytes of brotli (223,136 to 224,556) and removes none, since
nothing was deleted to pay for it.

Verified in the browser on a 12 x 12 Denver ground: shut by default with the
count in its summary, open it survives every redraw, hover, touch, arrow keys,
the unmeasured branch and a survey permalink all letter correctly, console
clean, exit 0. Module gates and the build pass.
